\begin{abstract}
Large language models are frequently asked to explain an answer they
have already produced, either in a separate inference step after the
answer is fixed (a \emph{post-hoc} explanation) or jointly with the
answer in one continuous decoding pass. We ask, under both
conditions, whether the resulting explanation faithfully communicates
the model's predictive uncertainty over its own answer, and whether
it discriminates between correct and incorrect answers when
justifying them. Comparing the two conditions directly lets us test
whether architectural separation between answer and explanation is
responsible for any uncertainty-communication failure we observe.
Across two question answering datasets spanning distinct task
structures (MedQA, StrategyQA), six model variants (base and
instruction-tuned Llama, Qwen, and Mistral), and a sample-based
estimator of predictive uncertainty, we find that, under both
conditions, explanation-communicated uncertainty is weakly and
inconsistently aligned with predictive uncertainty, and explanations
remain highly supportive of incorrect answers, with support scores
rarely falling below 60/100 even for answers independently
determined to be incorrect. Joint generation shows no consistent
improvement over post-hoc generation in uncertainty fidelity,
indicating that architectural separation alone is insufficient to
explain the phenomenon. Our results suggest that LLM explanations,
however they are generated, provide strong justificatory signals that
are only weakly coupled to the model's predictive uncertainty, with
direct consequences for user trust.
\end{abstract}

