\section{Introduction}

Large language models are frequently asked not just to answer a
question, but to explain that answer. Sometimes this explanation is
requested afterward, in a separate step, once the answer is already
fixed: a user asks a medical question, receives a diagnosis, and
asks ``why?''; a support agent asks a model to justify a
recommendation before passing it along. We call this the
\emph{post-hoc} case. The same question can equally be asked when
the model produces the answer and the explanation together in one
continuous pass, which we call the \emph{joint} case. We study both
throughout: every experiment in this paper is run under each
condition on the same models, datasets, and metrics
(\S\ref{sec:experiments}). Post-hoc explanation is where we ground
the paper's formal definition and theoretical characterization
(\S\ref{sec:definition}, \S\ref{sec:discussion}), since its
architecture admits a precise description; joint generation lets us
ask whether that architecture is what causes any gap we find, by
removing it and testing whether the gap remains. In both cases, we
ask the same thing: does the explanation tell a reader anything true
about how confident the model actually was?

This question matters because people use explanations to decide how
much to trust an answer, regardless of how that explanation was
produced. A hedged explanation reads as a signal the answer might be
wrong; a fluent, assertive one reads as a signal it is probably
right. If that signal is disconnected from the model's actual
reliability, a user's trust is being shaped by something other than
the thing it is supposed to track. We are not asking whether every
explanation must communicate uncertainty. We ask a narrower,
concrete question: when a model's own predictive distribution
indicates it is unreliable, does the explanation it generates,
post-hoc or jointly, say so?

We answer this empirically. We estimate each model's predictive
uncertainty using a sample-based semantic entropy estimator, and
elicit an explanation under each condition, scoring it with an
independent LLM judge along two dimensions: how much uncertainty it
communicates, and how strongly it argues the answer is correct.
Across three model families (Llama, Qwen, Mistral), base and
instruction-tuned, and two datasets with different answer structures
(StrategyQA, MedQA), we find a consistent pattern. Predictive
uncertainty is informative, reliably higher before incorrect answers
than correct ones. But communicated uncertainty barely tracks it,
in either condition. Meanwhile explanations keep defending the
answer regardless: even answers we can independently confirm are
wrong receive support scores of 60--90/100, only modestly lower than
correct answers. We then test whether this is simply an artifact of
generating the explanation in a separate pass that cannot see the
state that produced the answer, by comparing post-hoc against joint
generation directly. If the separate-pass architecture were the
cause, removing it should help. It does not: across twelve paired
comparisons, joint generation shows no consistent improvement (mean
$\Delta\rho = 0.024$, Wilcoxon $p=0.52$). Whatever drives the gap
between what the model knows and what its explanation says, it
survives giving the explanation full access to the answer-generation
process --- evidence for a trust hazard distinct from explanations
simply being wrong: a model can be internally uncertain and
externally assertive at once, and neither generation protocol closes
that gap.

Our contributions are: (1) a formal characterization of post-hoc
explanation as an architectural pattern (\S\ref{sec:definition}),
distinguishing it from chain-of-thought reasoning and joint
generation; (2) an empirical demonstration, across three model
families, two alignment regimes, two datasets, and both conditions,
that communicated uncertainty is weakly related to predictive
uncertainty while explanations remain highly supportive of incorrect
answers (\S\ref{sec:results}); (3) a mechanism ablation ruling out
separate-pass architecture as a sufficient explanation
(\S\ref{ssec:result-c}); and (4) an information-theoretic
characterization of the post-hoc channel and a stated hypothesis for
why the gap persists even when the architectural constraint does not
apply (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

