\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Faithful uncertainty expression.} The work closest to ours
asks whether a model's stated confidence matches its actual
reliability. \citet{kadavath2022language} show that large language
models can be well calibrated when evaluating the validity of their
own answers in a constrained format, but this measures a scalar
confidence judgment, not the free-text explanations people actually
read. \citet{lin2022teaching} similarly train models to verbalize a
numeric confidence, again separate from any explanation.
\citet{yona-etal-2024-large} come closest to our question by
comparing verbalized decisiveness against an independent confidence
estimate, but their object is the full response (answer plus any
hedging in the same utterance); we isolate the narrower case where an
explanation is elicited in a genuinely separate step, after an answer
is already fixed, and additionally ask whether the explanation
defends that answer regardless of its truth. \citet{liu2025metafaith}
extend this line with prompt-based interventions across a larger
model set, reporting limited effect on some models; our post-hoc and
joint conditions are complementary manipulations on the generation
side rather than the prompt side. \citet{mielke2022reducing} train
dialogue agents to linguistically calibrate their utterances, which
presupposes exactly the faithful confidence-to-language mapping we
find is largely absent without explicit training.
\citet{guo2017calibration} establish that neural networks are
frequently overconfident and popularize temperature scaling as a
correction; \citet{desai2020calibration} extend this to pre-trained
transformers; and \citet{ouyang2022training} show that RLHF-style
instruction tuning, which we use to distinguish base from
instruction-tuned models throughout, can itself degrade calibration.
Calibration in this sense asks whether a stated confidence
\emph{score} matches empirical accuracy; we ask a different question
--- whether a separately generated natural-language explanation's
rhetoric matches the model's own internal uncertainty --- so our
measurements (Spearman correlation, Mann--Whitney) are not
calibration metrics and should not be read as testing calibration.
Our base-versus-instruct comparison asks a related but distinct
question: not whether alignment changes calibration of the answer
itself, but whether it changes how faithfully an explanation reflects
whatever uncertainty remains, under either generation condition.

\paragraph{Faithfulness of reasoning and explanation.}
\citet{turpin2023language} show chain-of-thought explanations can be
systematically influenced by biasing features the model never
mentions, and \citet{lanham2023measuring} show larger models often
reason less faithfully even as accuracy improves. Both study a single
continuous generation in which the explanation precedes or
accompanies the answer; we study the complementary case in which the
explanation is requested only after the answer is fixed, and our
joint-generation ablation compares the two conditions directly within
the same models and datasets. \citet{atanasova2023faithfulness} and
\citet{siegel2024probabilities} develop tests for whether an
explanation's stated reasons are the reasons the model actually used;
\citet{parcalabescu2024measuring} raise related concerns about
conflating faithfulness with self-consistency. These works ask
whether an explanation's content is causally responsible for the
answer; we ask whether its \emph{rhetorical register} (how uncertain
or supportive it sounds) tracks the model's actual reliability,
independent of that causal question. \citet{lyu2024towards} survey
this broader literature, which our setting sits at the intersection
of with the confidence-calibration line above.

\paragraph{Uncertainty quantification.} We estimate predictive
uncertainty with a sample-based semantic entropy method in the
tradition of \citet{kuhn2023semantic} and
\citet{farquhar2024detecting}, who cluster repeated generations by
meaning and compute entropy over the resulting clusters;
\citet{kossen2024semantic} propose a cheaper probe-based
approximation, and \citet{xiong2024llms} benchmark verbalized and
sampling-based confidence-elicitation strategies across black-box
LLMs. None of this literature asks whether the resulting uncertainty
estimate is reflected in a separately generated explanation, which is
the gap we address. We report our main results on StrategyQA
\citep{geva-etal-2021-strategyqa}, a multi-hop commonsense benchmark,
and MedQA \citep{jin2021medqa}, a clinical board-exam benchmark with a
discrete five-option answer space chosen specifically because it
removes the need for a semantic-similarity threshold in estimating
predictive uncertainty (\S\ref{sec:measurement}), evaluating
Llama~3~\citep{grattafiori2024llama}, Qwen2.5
\citep{qwen2025qwen2}, and Mistral~7B \citep{jiang2023mistral}, each
in base and instruction-tuned form.

