\section{Experimental Setting}
\label{sec:experiments}

\paragraph{Data.} We use two question-answering datasets spanning
distinct task structures: \textbf{StrategyQA}
\citep{geva-etal-2021-strategyqa}, multi-hop commonsense reasoning
with binary yes/no answers, and \textbf{MedQA}
\citep{jin2021medqa}, discrete five-option clinical board-exam
questions. This span lets us test whether our findings are specific
to any one answer format or task type.

\paragraph{Models.} We evaluate three model families at the 7--8B
scale, each in both base and instruction-tuned form: Llama-3.1-8B
(base / Instruct), Qwen2.5-7B (base / Instruct), and Mistral-7B
(v0.1 base / v0.3 Instruct).

\paragraph{Methods.} For each question we generate under two
conditions. \textbf{Post-hoc} follows Definition~1 directly: the
answer is generated, and the explanation is elicited in a fresh
forward pass. \textbf{Joint} generates the answer and explanation in
a single continuous decoding pass, retaining shared context, as a
direct test of whether Definition~1's separation is the cause of any
observed uncertainty-communication failure.

\paragraph{Evaluation.} We report three analyses.
\textbf{Experiment A (Uncertainty Fidelity)}: the Spearman
correlation between $\widehat U$ and communicated
uncertainty, per model--dataset--condition combination, with 95\%
confidence intervals obtained by non-parametric bootstrap
($n{=}1000$ resamples). \textbf{Experiment B (Truth Sensitivity)}:
the \emph{Truth Sensitivity Gap},
$\mathrm{TSG} = \mathbb{E}[S\mid C{=}1] - \mathbb{E}[S\mid C{=}0]$,
the difference in mean support score between correct ($C{=}1$) and
incorrect ($C{=}0$) answers, tested via Mann--Whitney $U$ with
rank-biserial effect size and 95\% CI. Larger TSG indicates greater
truth discrimination; TSG near zero indicates that support is
largely uninformative about correctness. \textbf{Experiment C
(Mechanism)}: for each model--dataset pair with paired Post-hoc/Joint
data, we compute $\Delta\rho = \rho_{\text{Joint}} -
\rho_{\text{Post-hoc}}$ via paired bootstrap over questions
(preserving the pairing, since the same questions appear in both
conditions), and aggregate across all 12 pairs with a Wilcoxon
signed-rank test.

\paragraph{Multiple comparisons.} We report a large number of
individual hypothesis tests across model--dataset--condition
combinations (11 $\widehat U$-validity tests, 24 Experiment A
correlations, 12 post-hoc and 12 joint Experiment B comparisons, 12
Experiment C pairs). These are not independent replications in the
classical sense: combinations share model families, base/instruct
variants, overlapping questions within a dataset, and the same judge
models.
We therefore treat our conclusions as resting primarily on the
\emph{consistency and magnitude} of effects across configurations
rather than on counts of individually significant tests, and report
FDR-adjusted $q$-values alongside raw $p$-values in
Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-results}.

