\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Estimated predictive uncertainty contains information
about answer reliability}
\label{ssec:result-validity}

Before asking whether explanations communicate predictive
uncertainty, we first confirm that $\widehat U$ is a
meaningful estimate of it. Across 11 model--dataset combinations
tested (MedQA and StrategyQA, post-hoc condition), incorrect answers
exhibit significantly higher $\widehat U$ in 8 configurations, while
no configuration shows a significant reversal (full table in
Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-results}, Table~\ref{tab:ebse-validity}).
Thus, estimated predictive uncertainty contains answer-reliability
information across most of the settings we study, providing an
empirical basis for asking, in the next section, whether this
signal is reflected in explanations.

\subsection{Explanation-communicated uncertainty is weakly and
inconsistently aligned with predictive uncertainty}
\label{ssec:result-a}

Figure~\ref{fig:forest} shows the Spearman correlation between
$\widehat U$ and communicated uncertainty for all 24
model--dataset--condition combinations tested. Higher judge scores
indicate greater communicated uncertainty; uncertainty fidelity
therefore predicts a positive correlation between $\widehat U$ and
judge score. Point estimates instead cluster near zero: all observed
correlations have small magnitudes ($|\rho|\le 0.236$), with the
largest observed magnitude $|\rho|=0.236$ (StrategyQA,
Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, post-hoc, $p=0.0008$). The majority of 95\%
confidence intervals include zero. We do not treat the count of
individually significant versus non-significant tests as the primary
evidence here, since the 24 configurations are not independent
replications (\S\ref{sec:experiments}); rather, across three model
families, two alignment regimes, and two generation conditions, the
association remains weak in magnitude throughout, indicating that
communicated uncertainty is at best a weak proxy for variation in
the model's predictive uncertainty. Full per-configuration
correlations with bootstrap CIs are plotted in
Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-results}, Figure~\ref{fig:forest}.

\subsection{Explanations remain highly supportive of incorrect
answers}
\label{ssec:result-b}

Figure~\ref{fig:support} and Table~\ref{tab:tsg} report mean support
scores for correct and incorrect answers under the post-hoc
condition. The central finding is the absolute level of support for
incorrect answers, not the significance of the gap: across all
twelve model--dataset combinations, answers determined to be
incorrect (\S\ref{sec:measurement}) receive support scores of
62--91/100. In several cases this is nearly indistinguishable from
the support given to correct answers (e.g.\ MedQA, Qwen Base:
$S_{\text{correct}}=93.9$ vs.\ $S_{\text{incorrect}}=91.0$,
$p=0.495$); we read this null result as one of the clearest
instances of the phenomenon we document, not as an absence of
effect. Where the gap (Truth Sensitivity Gap, TSG) does reach
significance --- nine of twelve comparisons
(Table~\ref{tab:tsg}) --- the magnitude varies across configurations
(Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-results}, Table~\ref{tab:effect-sizes}).
Crucially, even configurations exhibiting substantial
correct--incorrect separation retain high absolute support for
incorrect answers, and incorrect-answer support never approaches the
low end of the scale in any comparison. We regard this as the
paper's most direct trustworthiness finding: an explanation providing
60--90/100 support for an incorrect answer does not merely fail to
hedge, it actively constructs the appearance of justification, even
though incorrect answers, at the population level, are associated
with elevated
predictive uncertainty in most configurations
(\S\ref{ssec:result-validity}).

