\subsection{Removing the separate-pass architecture does not
restore uncertainty fidelity}
\label{ssec:result-c}

Definition~1 identifies the separation between answer and
explanation generation as an architectural constraint on what
information can reach $\mathbf{E}$. We test whether removing this
constraint --- generating $\mathbf{A}$ and $\mathbf{E}$ jointly, in a
single decoding pass with shared context --- improves uncertainty
fidelity. Figure~\ref{fig:c1} shows $\Delta\rho = \rho_{\text{Joint}}
- \rho_{\text{Post-hoc}}$ for each of the twelve paired
model--dataset combinations available (identical question sets in
both conditions; \S\ref{sec:appendix-implementation} reports pairing
diagnostics). Only one of twelve individual comparisons reaches
significance (MedQA, Mistral-7B-v0.1, $\Delta\rho=0.200$, 95\% CI
$[0.021, 0.386]$); the aggregate distribution shows no consistent
direction (7 of 12 pairs favor Joint, 5 favor Post-hoc; mean
$\Delta\rho=0.024$, median $=0.037$; Wilcoxon signed-rank $p=0.52$).
Per-pair estimates with bootstrap CIs are plotted in
Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-results}, Figure~\ref{fig:c1}.

We read this null result as evidence \emph{against} the sufficiency
of architectural separation as an explanation for F2
(\S\ref{ssec:result-a}): granting the explanation-generation process
computational continuity with answer generation does not reliably
improve how faithfully the resulting explanation communicates
uncertainty. We discuss the implications of this finding for
characterizing the phenomenon, as distinct from explaining it, in
\S\ref{sec:discussion}.

