\section{An Information-Theoretic Characterization}
\label{sec:discussion}

Sections~\ref{ssec:result-validity}--\ref{ssec:result-c} establish
three empirical findings: estimated predictive uncertainty carries
information about answer reliability (F1); that information is
weakly and inconsistently reflected in communicated uncertainty
(F2); explanations remain highly supportive of incorrect answers
(F3); and removing the architectural separation between answer and
explanation generation produces no consistent improvement
(Experiment C). These findings are established under both the
post-hoc and joint conditions. The characterization below, by
contrast, is scoped deliberately to the post-hoc condition: Definition~1
gives an exact conditional-independence property that holds for
post-hoc generation by construction, and Proposition~1 is a direct
consequence of that property. Joint generation, while tested
empirically throughout, does not satisfy the same property --- it
preserves computational continuity with $Z$ rather than eliminating
access to it (\S\ref{sec:definition}) --- so we do not claim
Proposition~1 or the discussion that follows applies to it. We
characterize the information-theoretic structure of the post-hoc
channel that Definition~1 describes, and present this as a
\emph{characterization} of the channel, not as an explanation for
why F2 and F3 occur --- Experiment C shows directly that the
constraint identified below is not, by itself, sufficient to account
for the empirical pattern.

\subsection{Architectural Information Bottleneck}
Recall that a post-hoc explanation $\mathbf{E}$ is generated
conditioned only on $\mathbf{Q}$ and the realized text of
$\mathbf{A}$, with no access to the latent decoder state $Z$ beyond
what $\mathbf{A}$ encodes.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Proposition 1 (Architectural Information Bottleneck).} Under
Definition~1, $Z \to \mathbf{A} \to \mathbf{E}$ forms a Markov chain
conditional on $\mathbf{Q}$, and consequently
\[
I(Z;\mathbf{E}\mid \mathbf{Q}) \le I(Z;\mathbf{A}\mid \mathbf{Q}).
\]
\end{quote}

\noindent\emph{Proof.} By Definition~1,
$P_\theta(\mathbf{E}\mid Z,\mathbf{A},\mathbf{Q}) =
P_\theta(\mathbf{E}\mid\mathbf{A},\mathbf{Q})$: $\mathbf{E}$ is
conditionally independent of $Z$ given $\mathbf{A}$ and
$\mathbf{Q}$, exactly the condition defining this Markov chain. The
(conditional) data processing inequality gives the stated bound
directly. \hfill$\blacksquare$

No post-hoc explanation can carry more information about the
model's latent state, for a given query, than survives in the text
of the answer it explains --- a statement about the computational
structure in Definition~1, not an empirical claim.

This bound does not, however, directly constrain predictive
uncertainty. $U(\mathbf{Q}) = \mathcal{H}_{\text{sem}}(P_\theta(\mathbf{A}\mid\mathbf{Q}))$
is a property of the model's distribution over answers to a given
$\mathbf{Q}$, not a deterministic function of the single realized
$Z$ for that query. Conditioning on $\mathbf{Q}$, as Proposition~1
does, therefore yields no bound relating $U(\mathbf{Q})$ to
$\mathbf{E}$: $U(\mathbf{Q})$ is fixed once $\mathbf{Q}$ is fixed, so
no further mutual-information statement follows from Proposition~1
alone. Whether post-hoc explanations preserve variation in
predictive uncertainty \emph{across queries} is consequently an
empirical question, which Experiment A (\S\ref{ssec:result-a})
tests directly.

\subsection{Justification Preference Hypothesis}
Proposition~1 characterizes the information constraint imposed by
post-hoc generation, while \S6.2 establishes that this constraint
does not itself determine how faithfully predictive uncertainty is
communicated. Neither explains F3: why explanations remain strongly
supportive even of answers that are demonstrably incorrect. We do
not derive an explanation for this from information theory, and
state it instead as a plain hypothesis about the elicitation
procedure itself.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Hypothesis 1 (Justification Preference).} Standard post-hoc
explanation elicitation conditions the model on an already-selected
answer and requests a rationale for that answer. This elicitation
explicitly requests answer-consistent explanation generation, but
imposes no explicit requirement that explanation language preserve
information about the model's predictive uncertainty.
\end{quote}

This hypothesis predicts that explanations may remain strongly
supportive even when the selected answer is incorrect, because the
elicitation procedure provides no explicit instruction to reduce
support when the underlying predictive state is uncertain. This does
not require support to be independent of correctness --- our data
show a significant gap in most configurations (\S\ref{ssec:result-b})
--- only that whatever gap exists is not large enough to bring
incorrect-answer support down to a level that would signal
unreliability to a reader. Experiment B tests this directly.

\subsection{What Experiment C Establishes}
Experiment~C (\S\ref{ssec:result-c}) tests whether removing the
separate-pass architecture behind Proposition~1's bound is
\emph{sufficient} to restore uncertainty fidelity, and finds that it
is not. This does not overturn Proposition~1 --- the bound still
holds under Definition~1's post-hoc condition --- but it rules out
architectural separation as a sufficient explanation for the weak
uncertainty fidelity we observe, leaving elicitation-induced
justification preference (Hypothesis~1) as one plausible mechanism
consistent with the pattern: an explanation-generation process
without an incentive to preserve $U(\mathbf{Q})$ may fail to do so
even when answer and explanation generation share computational
continuity, since shared continuity with $Z$ does not itself supply
that incentive. Table~\ref{tab:theory-map} (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-discussion})
summarizes how each theoretical claim maps onto its corresponding
test.

