\subsection{FDR-Corrected Significance}
Given the number of hypothesis tests reported across overlapping
configurations (\S\ref{sec:experiments}), we additionally report
Benjamini--Hochberg FDR-corrected $q$-values at $\alpha=0.05$ for our
three main significance tables. For the post-hoc Truth Sensitivity
Gap (Table~\ref{tab:tsg}, $n=12$), 9 of 12 comparisons have raw
$p<0.05$; 8 of 12 remain significant after FDR correction (the
MedQA Llama-Instruct and Qwen-Base comparisons, and the StrategyQA
Mistral-Instruct and Mistral-Base comparisons, do not survive
correction). For the joint-condition TSG (Table~\ref{tab:tsg-joint},
$n=12$), 8 of 12 have raw $p<0.05$, and all 8 remain significant
after correction. For the $\widehat U$-validity check
(Table~\ref{tab:ebse-validity}, $n=11$), 8 of 11 have raw $p<0.05$;
7 of 11 remain significant after correction, with MedQA Mistral-Base
($p=0.038$, $q=0.052$) no longer surviving. In all three tables, the
comparisons that lose significance under FDR correction are
precisely those with the smallest raw effect sizes, and no
previously non-significant comparison becomes significant; the
qualitative pattern reported in the main text --- higher estimated
uncertainty for incorrect answers in most configurations, and
persistently high incorrect-answer support throughout --- is
unchanged after correction.

